WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.350
THE STATE OF PRINTERS:
Printers are the worst of the worst.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:08.511
They struggle to connect to the wifi, they
constantly have paper jams, and don’t get

00:00:08.511 --> 00:00:11.780
me started at the ludicrous price of ink.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.400
It seems that printers are one of the few
tech industries that have been completely

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.920
left behind.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.720
I mean you can buy full on computers and smartphones
for less than the price of a new printer.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.990
Yet, printers can’t even do the one task
that their built for: printing.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.540
It’s easy to blame the printer industry
as a whole and say that printer companies

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.620
are simply unimaginative and not innovative.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:41.700
But, the issue with printers is a lot more
deep rooted than uninspired companies and

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.320
lack luster products.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.800
The real reason printers are so crappy is
due to decades of hyper focus on maximizing

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.250
revenue through one avenue: ink.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.899
It’s not uncommon for printer ink to exceed
$80 per ounce.

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:02.660
For perspective, even human blood only costs
$17 per ounce and silver costs $34 per ounce.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:10.120
In fact, selling ink is so profitable, that
it accounted for 63% of all of HP Inc’s

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.200
profits in 2019.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.240
But, it wasn’t always like this.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.049
In fact, 50 to 60 years ago, printers were
the creme of the crop.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:22.110
They were the best that the tech world had
to offer and they worked beautifully.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.509
Back in the 1960s, Xerox literally made a
commercial showcasing how even a chimpanzee

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:29.759
could operate a photocopier.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:34.040
Printer operators were so insulted after this
airing that Xerox would decide to cancel the

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.229
commercial just one day later.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.619
But, that doesn’t take away from the message
of the commercial.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:46.040
Printers were once easy enough for trained
chimpanzees to use, but today even trained

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.970
rocket scientists at NASA can’t figure out
how to operate these darn things.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.799
So, what happened?

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:57.979
How did printers go from being the forefront
of the tech industry and the entire business

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:03.570
of one of the biggest companies in the world
Xerox to being the laughing stock of the tech

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:09.180
industry and the endless frustration of hundreds
of millions of office workers around the world?

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:15.060
Well, sit back and grab a stress ball because
this is exactly how printers became the bane

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.960
of our existence.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.019
WHEN PRINTERS WERE GOOD:
Taking a look back, printers are by no means

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:24.440
a new invention.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:30.940
It’s theorized that printers may date as
far back as 3500 BC to the Persians and Mesopotamians

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.660
who used cylinders to seal documents similar
to what we do today.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.370
But, the modern printing press didn’t come
around till the year 1440 when a German goldsmith

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.190
named Johannes Gutenberg invented the movable-type
printing press.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.930
At the time, this was a true revolution as
the alternative was hand printing which was

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.780
limited ot about 40 pages per day.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.040
Gutenberg’s printing press put that number
to shame as it could produce 3600 pages every

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.220
single day.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.319
Over the next couple hundred years, the printing
press became the gold standard for printing

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:04.800
religious literature, newspapers, and books.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.319
But eventually moving into the 1900s, there
arose a new purpose for printing.

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:15.129
People and corporations alike needed an easy
way to copy existing documents at a small

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:16.129
scale.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:20.140
They wanted to have additional copies of their
bond and land certificates and their employment

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.140
contract.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.310
But, while the printing press was great at
replicating a given piece of text like a Bible

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.260
at scale, it wasn't all the great when it
came to replicating individual documents and

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.260
pages.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.840
Chester Carlson, a law student in the late
1930s knew this pain all too well.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.440
You see, Chester couldn’t afford to buy
law books outright, so he would go to the

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.390
library and painstakingly transcribe books
onto sheets of paper.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.350
It was during one of these arduous copying
sessions that he came across a scientific

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:52.000
article by a Hungarian physicist named Pal
Selenyi.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.670
Selenyi loosely described how you could use
physics and more specifically electrophotography

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.150
to duplicate documents more effectively.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.424
It was really more of a broad theoretical
paper, but Carlson was determined to make

00:03:59.424 --> 00:04:00.424
it a reality.

00:04:00.424 --> 00:04:04.080
Carlson would begin trying to do electrophotography
in his apartment which led to a bunch of smoke,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.960
odd aromas, and even explosions.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.209
His neighbors probably suspected that he was
smoking up something else, but Carlson persisted

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:17.060
and with the help of a friend, he would produce
the following piece of text on October 22,

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.209
1938.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.930
Carlson was sure to patent this technology
as soon as possible and eventually, he would

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.460
partner with an up and coming company called
the Haloid Company.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:32.250
And together, they would create what we all
know as Xerox today, but it was by no means

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.260
an easy road.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.240
You see, there was one major flaw with Carlson’s
invention.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.250
It was extraordinarily difficult to use.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:45.270
To be honest, I think we’d agree that electrophotography
is probably easier than what we have today,

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.490
but nonetheless, Carlson wanted to make it
even easier.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:53.800
With backing from Xerox, Carlson would spend
the next 21 years of his life trying to simplify

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.889
each and every step of the process.

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:03.190
And eventually in 1959, he would crack the
code with the Xerox 914.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:07.889
This was the photocopier that was so easy
to use that even a Chimpanzee could use it

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:10.530
leading us into the golden era of printers

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:17.520
UNREASONABLY EXPENSIVE:
It may be hard to believe that printers were

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.770
actually good at one point in time, so I’m
sure you’ll be relieved to hear that there

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.819
was a massive caveat.

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:31.010
Yes, the Xerox 914 and all of the photocopiers
and printers that followed in the 60s, 70s,

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:37.520
and even 80s were extraordinary, but it wasn’t
just the product that was extraordinary; it

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.509
was also the price tag.

00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:44.740
The cost to make a Xerox 914 was simply so
expensive that they didn’t even try to sell

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:45.740
it.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.430
Instead, they resorted to renting them out
to local photocopying shops and offices for

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.280
$25 per month or $238 today.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.319
Additionally, customers would have to pay
10 cents per copy or 96 cents today.

00:05:57.319 --> 00:06:01.470
Customers were also responsible for bringing
their own paper and ink which usually ran

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.139
about 5 cents per copy.

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:07.940
So, all in, customers were paying over a dollar
for every single page they were printing.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.189
The only people who were able to get around
renting was the US government as they were

00:06:09.189 --> 00:06:10.189
dead set on buying these outright.

00:06:10.189 --> 00:06:15.610
They would get their wish but it would cost
them an absolute fortune coming in at $27,500.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:21.110
That’s the same as $262,000 today.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:26.660
Photocopying was obviously not cheap, but
it was more than worth it for important documents.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:32.139
I mean the consequences of losing a land deed
or bond certificate were far worse than spending

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:34.099
a dollar to have an extra copy.

00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:41.100
This proved to be insanely profitable for
Xerox and by 1970, Xerox would become a Fortune

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.960
100 company which for a tech company was at
the time unheard of.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.130
With all this revenue coming in, Xerox would
invest a crap ton of money into research and

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:52.810
development, and they actually came up with
outstanding inventions.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:58.190
Not only would they make printers way better,
but they would literally invent laptops, the

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:02.389
internet, coding languages, text editors,
and so much more.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:07.389
Now, they were so gung ho on printers that
they would end up giving all of these away,

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:09.590
but that’s a whole nother story.

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:14.790
The point is that they were truly maintaining
printers at the forefront of technology, but

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:20.229
with that, they were also charging an arm
and a leg and keeping up the renting model.

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:25.000
For obvious reasons, having people pay per
copy was a far better business decision for

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.509
Xerox than trying to sell printers outright.

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:31.690
But, the reality was that things were changing
once again.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:36.370
The need to photocopy was quickly being replaced
by the need to print digital documents.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:41.169
Fortunately, it was Xerox who invented text
editors and the ability to print text documents,

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:44.751
so they could serve this need as well, but
there was one problem, you had to go to a

00:07:44.751 --> 00:07:46.150
Xerox shop.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:51.550
If you were using a computer cafe, it made
sense to use the Xerox printer in the building.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:56.259
But, while computer and internet cafes were
super popular back in the day, people would

00:07:56.259 --> 00:07:59.900
eventually ditch these for personal computers
in the comfort of their homes.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:05.370
And traveling to Xerox shops just to print
stuff out quickly became an inconvenience.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:10.680
HP, Epson, and Canon were all watching this
from the sidelines, and they decided that

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.729
this was the perfect time to strike.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:19.760
THE DEMISE OF PRINTERS:
The first mass market residential inkjet printer

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.330
was the HP Deskjet 500 which came out in 1988.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:28.490
Like its Xerox predecessors, this was truly
an engineering masterpiece.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:34.810
In fact, it was so good that journalists like
Scott Merrill were raving about 22 years later.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:38.320
“The HP DeskJet 500 was a workhorse.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.120
It never failed me in college, or beyond.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.160
I printed a lot, and never had a paper jam.”

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.459
“Everything I threw at the DeskJet 500,
it printed.

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:49.459
Everything.”

00:08:49.459 --> 00:08:52.560
But, if printers were so good, what happened?

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.960
Well, it turns out that asking people to pay
$1000 for a printer in 1988 was a pretty large

00:08:58.960 --> 00:08:59.960
ask.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:04.899
With budget computer brands like Dell popping
up, you could literally buy an entire computer

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:09.160
for that price, so why would you spend the
same amount on just getting a printing machine?

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.160
Well, the answer was that most people wouldn’t.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.459
If these companies really wanted to create
a personal printer market, they had to make

00:09:14.459 --> 00:09:19.790
printers cheaper, way cheaper, but this was
much easier said then done.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:23.970
Just because computers offered better value
for your money didn’t mean that it was any

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.610
cheaper to design or produce printers.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.899
But, that was irrelevant to end consumers.

00:09:29.899 --> 00:09:33.830
If you wanted to appeal to them, it was up
to you to figure out how to make printers

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:36.180
cheap enough for everyday people.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.610
With all these mega companies working on this,
you would think that some engineer somewhere

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:44.870
would make a monumental breakthrough that
substantially simplified printers and made

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:48.370
them far cheaper, but this never happened.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.430
Instead, printer manufacturers just decided
to sell printers for a loss.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:57.870
They figured they could just make the money
on the ink, and somehow 30 years later, they’re

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.850
still using the same strategy and they’ve
only gotten more aggressive.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:06.610
The reason that printers are affordable today
is not because they’re cheaper to make but

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:10.279
because companies are willing to take bigger
and bigger losses.

00:10:10.279 --> 00:10:15.339
But, with that being said, when you’re selling
a product for a loss, you’re naturally not

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:20.360
going to invest into R&amp;D, testing, reliability,
or ease of use.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:26.550
Instead, you’re gonna put out an MVP or
a minimum viable product, and this is the

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:30.280
reality of the entire consumer printer industry
today.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.230
The reason printers are so bad is because
every company is trying to shrink an extremely

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.839
complex product into an MVP.

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:40.720
I mean just think about.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.520
A printer has to put millions of dots onto
a piece of paper within a matter of seconds

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:46.520
seconds.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:52.269
There’s no way that such a complex product
can work as an MVP that’s sold for a loss.

00:10:52.269 --> 00:10:56.720
Printer companies should be thinking about
how to engineer better printers, but given

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:00.940
that that’s not where the money is, printer
companies have been spending all of their

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:06.800
efforts on how to sell as much ink as possible,
and this has led to some pretty anti-consumer

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:07.800
inventions.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:12.019
Some companies will straight up prevent you
from using counterfeit ink through software,

00:11:12.019 --> 00:11:17.190
but even if they don’t go that far, they’ll
make sure that you feel that pain.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:22.360
For example, the printheads of many printers
are actually located on the ink cartridge.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:27.000
This means that the quality of your print
will only be as good as the ink you buy.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.120
So, if you bought some counterfeit cartridges
and noticed that the quality went down substantially,

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.320
this is probably why.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:40.140
A BLEAK FUTURE:
Looking forward, the future of printers isn’t

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.180
looking any brighter because printers as a
whole are going extinct.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:48.180
Here’s the thing, one of main reasons that
printers became so popular in the first place

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.399
was because of trust.

00:11:50.399 --> 00:11:53.769
People didn’t trust computers as much as
they trusted themselves.

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:58.019
They wanted to have hard copies of important
documents, they wanted real signatures that

00:11:58.019 --> 00:12:00.150
felt authentic, they wanted something real.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:05.100
But, today, printers are becoming irrelevant
for the exact same reason trust.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:08.770
This time though, it’s themselves that people
don’t trust.

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:12.329
People and companies alike don’t want to
have filing cabinets full of documents.

00:12:12.329 --> 00:12:17.020
They don’t want to hold onto 200 page contracts
that are fatter than books.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:22.120
And they trust the cloud and 7 backups across
the world more than a singular hard copy.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:26.950
At this point, hard copies are more of a formality
than anything.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:31.850
Everything from buying a house to completing
an acquisition can be done completely digitally

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.770
and this is what people actually prefer.

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:39.079
So, with that being said, it doesn’t make
sense for anyone to double down on the printer

00:12:39.079 --> 00:12:41.630
industry and try to make it better.

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:47.070
The reality is that printers are going extinct
and the last memory that we’ll have of them

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:48.070
is good riddens.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.880
Do you hate printers?

